{"body":"We currently reuse the radicle ed25519 key to create the iroh endpoint. The appeal is minimalism: one key, one identity, and since the endpoint ID is derivable from the public key, bare `iroh://` URIs can resolve straight from the author DID.\n\nA patch to decouple the two keys ([c884af8](rad:patch/c884af86bfb218bd0cc98a2beadc8948f487a9f1)) was explored and archived. This issue tracks the topic so we can revisit when the trade-offs shift.\n\n## Costs of reusing the radicle key\n\n- **ssh-agent contract.** radicle keeps the private key out of process memory by delegating signing to ssh-agent. Reusing the key for iroh breaks that — establishing the endpoint requires the raw private key, so we prompt for the passphrase and load it into memory.\n- **Future-proofing.** radicle itself plans to decouple the user DID from the node ID in the mid-term; reusing the radicle key now means reworking this again later.\n- **Blast radius.** Separate keys for separate purposes is generally good hygiene — a leak of the iroh key shouldn't compromise the radicle identity, and vice versa.\n\nNote: serving the same DID from multiple endpoints (e.g. radicle-desktop and an always-on CLI host) is not itself blocked by the current design — `iroh://` URIs can embed a full endpoint ID, so a DID can already point at more than one endpoint when the key is not reused.\n\n## Costs of separating the keys\n\n- A second key to manage and back up alongside the radicle key.\n- Endpoint IDs can no longer be derived from the DID, so the full endpoint ID must be written into `iroh://` location URIs (with a fallback path for bare URIs produced by older CLIs).\n\n## Triggers to revisit\n\n- Upstream radicle decouples user DID from node ID.\n- An ssh-agent-friendly path for iroh endpoint setup becomes available.\n- Operational pain from passphrase prompting / in-memory key handling grows.","type":"comment"}